    <!-- Cultural Stories -->
    <string name="story_title_1">The History of Beeralu</string>
    <string name="story_body_1">Beeralu lace making is a traditional craft that has been passed down through generations in the southern coastal areas of Sri Lanka. Rooted in the colonial era, it remains a testament to the intricate skill and patience of local artisans, preserving a unique heritage that defines the cultural identity of Galle.</string>
    <string name="story_title_2">Priyani’s Vision</string>
    <string name="story_body_2">After the devastating 2004 Tsunami, master artisan M.B. Priyani spearheaded the revival of Beeralu lace in Galle. Her dedication helped rebuild the lives of many women, transforming a struggling craft into a symbol of resilience and empowerment for the community.</string>
    <string name="story_title_3">Portuguese Origins</string>
    <string name="story_body_3">Although deeply integrated into Sri Lankan culture, Beeralu lace making was originally introduced by the Portuguese in the 16th century. Over hundreds of years, local artisans adapted these techniques, infusing them with Sri Lankan motifs and rhythms to create the distinct \'Galle Lace\' we know today.</string>

    <!-- Onboarding -->
    <string name="onboard_title_1">Learn Beeralu Lace Making</string>
    <string name="onboard_desc_1">Master the ancient craft of Galle\'s Beeralu lace with AR-guided hand tracking and expert templates.</string>
    <string name="onboard_title_2">Guided by Master Artisans</string>
    <string name="onboard_desc_2">Practice alongside expert recordings from master artisan M.B. Priyani. Get real-time accuracy feedback as you move.</string>
    <string name="onboard_title_3">Preserve Heritage Together</string>
    <string name="onboard_desc_3">Join a community dedicated to preserving Sri Lanka\'s 600-year-old Beeralu tradition for future generations.</string>

